Scott 1976, Theorem 4.1, the complete-lattice half. The greatest fixed point of a Scott-continuous map below the intersection of a family of fixed points is itself a fixed point.

Let z be that greatest fixed point below the intersection. Its image meets the intersection in z, and z is contained in the intersection. The image of z is contained in the intersection: each member of the family lies above z, so monotonicity carries the image into the image of that member, which equals the member. The intersection of the image with the family intersection is therefore the image, and the fixed-intersection equation becomes the fixed-point equation.
